.TH BOOT 7
.SH NAME
boot \- how SIEOS starts, from the firmware to the login prompt
.SH DESCRIPTION
.SS 1. Loading
.TP
.B x86-64, QEMU
QEMU (or GRUB) finds the kernel's multiboot header and loads it at 1 MiB,
with the boot modules (init, con, usb, vblk, fs) as separate files
.RB ( "\-initrd" ).
.TP
.B x86-64, a PC (UEFI)
the firmware starts SIEOS's own loader, EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI, from a USB
key's EFI partition (or \eEFI\eSIEOS\e on a drive SIEOS was installed on).
It reads
.BR boot.cfg (5),
loads KERNEL.BIN and the modules, sets the screen mode, finds the ACPI
tables, leaves the firmware and jumps to the kernel's 64-bit entry.
.TP
.B arm64 (QEMU virt, Raspberry Pi 4 and 5)
QEMU or the Pi's firmware (config.txt) loads the kernel image (kernel8.img
on a Pi's card) and passes a device tree; the boot modules are one archive,
modules.img (sieos.mod on a card, loaded at 32 MiB), passed as the initrd.
.SS 2. The kernel
The entry
.RB ( boot.S )
builds the first page tables and switches to 64-bit mode (x86-64), or drops
from EL2 to EL1 and turns the MMU on (arm64).
The platform code turns the loader's information or the device tree into
one description of memory, CPUs, devices and modules.
.B kernel_main
sets up memory and the first CPU, starts the first boot module,
.BR init ,
starts the other CPUs, and from then on only runs processes.
Boot options:
.BI cpus= N
(start at most N CPUs),
.BI pcores= N
(pretend CPUs 0..N\-1 are P-cores),
.BI root= GUID
(SIEOS's partition).
.SS 3. The supervisor
.BR init (8)
is the only program the kernel starts.
It alone may give device rights and start the other boot modules, which
stay in memory so that a crashed disk driver can come back.
From its table it starts, in order: the console
.RB ( con (4)),
the USB server
.RB ( usb (4)),
the disk
.RB ( vblk (4)),
the files
.RB ( fs (4)),
then from /bin: login, the boot log
.RB ( "dmesg \-w" ),
on x86-64 the ACPI server, the touchpad and touch screen drivers, the
desktop
.RB ( atlas (4)),
Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, and
.BR sshd (8)
if it is turned on.
The accounts, the network, sound, the pins, pipes and the assistant start on
demand, when a program first looks their port up.
A server without its hardware ends with status 0 and is not started again.
.SS 4. Logging in
.BR login (8)
asks the accounts server
.RB ( auth (8))
whether accounts exist.
On a new disk there are none: the desktop's welcome screen creates them, or,
with no screen, the terminal does.
Then login reads a name and a password and asks auth, which checks the
password, opens the user's encrypted home, and starts the user's shell
.RB ( "sh \-l" )
as a child of login, on its terminal.
login waits for the shell to end, then shows the prompt again.
On the desktop, the login screen does the same, and each terminal window
runs a shell as the user.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/boot.log
the log since boot, saved 20 s, 1, 2 and 5 minutes after starting (up to the
console's first login prompt; root only)
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR boot.cfg (5),
.BR init (8),
.BR svc (8),
.BR intro (4),
.BR security (7),
.BR dmesg (1)
